Beautiful and peaceful game. Little bit overwhelming at first (what to do) but once you get the hang of how to surive it becomes fun and makes you think about the strategic and ethic decisions you can make.

+ Interesting story: scavenge to survive with ethical choices;
+ Multiple strategies: grab/steal what you can or produce and trade;
+ Well chosen and peaceful audio/soundtracks;
+ Variance in gameplay: different locations, characters, changing weather, radio news;
+ Beautiful graphics and styling;
- User created workshops have compatibility problems due game updates;
- Some game bugs (one crash to desktop, one time unable to move a character anymore).

[h1]This War of Mine[/h1]

A must-buy for anyone who's even remotely interested in strategy and/or good video games. At first I was a bit overwhelmed by the different elements of the game, and this affected my first play-through to the point where failing made me feel genuinely sad. Not because I didn't "win", but rather because of the unjustness of the conditions that caused my survivors to get sick or depressed and eventually die. And after 30 or so hours and getting better at the game, I started wondering if that might have been the point: everything doesn't neatly fall into place for civilians in wartime—death is constant. And maybe that's what this game teaches: yes, as you play, things start to feel more and more enjoyable, so to speak, but first you have to go through a harsher, more honest representation of war.

[b]Pros:[/b]
[list]
[*]Beautiful artwork, tense atmosphere;
[*]Tackles important themes in a surprisingly harsh but honest light;
[*]Decent amount of locations to explore, along with new characters and simply getting used to the mechanics adds to replayability;
[*]Decisions have tangible consequences;
[*]Gameplay adapts to your play style;
[*]Great soundtrack;
[/list]

[b]Cons:[/b]
[list]
[*]Lack of tutorial can make things a bit overwhelming (if not downright depressing) at first;
[*]Not a lot of [i]scenario[/i] variations to increase replay value, in contrast to the above.
[/list]

reccomending this as an artwork, not gameplay (to be honest, the gameplay mechanics get very rote, not a game in the traditional sense)

